---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { profile } from "../../components/town/profile";
import { ktoCaseStudy as study } from "../../data/ktoCaseStudy";
import "../../styles/case-study.css";
---

<Layout title="KTO sportsbook — Jack Winter" description={study.summary}>
  <div class="case-study">
    <nav class="case-nav" aria-label="Case study navigation">
      <a href="/portfolio#experience">← Experience</a>
      <a href="/#profile">Meet Jack ↗</a>
    </nav>
    <main id="main">
      <header class="case-intro">
        <p class="case-eyebrow">KTO Group · Jun 2025 — Present</p>
        <h1>{study.title}</h1>
        <p class="case-lead">{study.summary}</p>
        <p>My role: Senior Front-End Developer · Remote</p>
      </header>
      <section aria-labelledby="results-title">
        <h2 id="results-title">Performance results</h2>
        <p class="case-qualification">{study.qualification}</p>
        <dl class="case-metrics">
          {study.metrics.map((metric) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{metric.label}</dt>
              <dd>{metric.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="context-title">
        <h2 id="context-title">The work</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            KTO operates a sportsbook and online casino in the Brazilian market.
            I led the frontend transformation from a third-party hosted product
            to a custom platform, within a business serving 1.4M active users
            and 5B bets placed.
          </p>
          <p>
            I owned the frontend architecture and core customer journeys. The
            performance work covered rendering, bundles, service loading and
            real-time data processing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="loading-title">
        <h2 id="loading-title">Getting the first view on screen</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            I changed the loading sequence to fetch and render the initial
            events, main markets and odds before setting up live updates and
            progressively subscribing the markets. This reduced the work that
            had to finish before the first view could appear.
          </p>
          <p>
            Alongside that, I split code, lazy-loaded non-critical services,
            removed unused translations and used lighter dependency builds.
            Deferring work can make a feature’s first use slower, and the
            initial market snapshot still needs to transition correctly into
            live state.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="return-title">
        <h2 id="return-title">Avoiding repeated setup on return visits</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            Returning to the sportsbook could repeat expensive Kambi client
            initialisation. Keeping the initialised client alive for reuse
            avoided that bootstrap work and made the tested return journey
            around 11 times faster. This result applies to leaving the
            sportsbook and coming back, rather than a first visit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Reuse retains state and memory, so session validity, the client’s
            container, subscriptions and listeners all matter to a usable
            return.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="icons-title">
        <h2 id="icons-title">Making consumers pay for one icon</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            Importing one icon from our shared package’s root entry retained
            roughly 65 KB gzip. Inspecting the built output showed that
            generated icon components were flattened into the root module;
            marking the package side-effect-free had not achieved the expected
            isolation.
          </p>
          <p>
            I changed the package API to generate isolated per-icon entry
            points. A representative single-icon import then retained roughly
            0.3–0.4 KB gzip. I added a bundle budget and structural tests to
            protect that behaviour. This was a separate package measurement, not
            the overall sportsbook’s transferred-data result.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="architecture-title">
        <h2 id="architecture-title">Separating responsibilities</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            I designed a modular sportsbook architecture across a pnpm/Turborepo
            monorepo spanning 27 packages and 22 modules, separating reusable
            UI, domain logic, data providers and third-party integrations.
          </p>
          <ul class="case-modules" aria-label="Architecture responsibilities">
            <li>Reusable UI</li>
            <li>Domain logic</li>
            <li>Data providers</li>
            <li>Third-party integrations</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Keeping high-frequency processing off the main thread</h3>
          <p>
            The real-time pipeline used browser Web Workers, Socket.IO and
            Zustand. Heavier normalisation, filtering and sorting ran in a
            worker, with batched snapshots containing the fields each subscriber
            needed sent back to main-thread stores for React to consume.
          </p>
          <p>
            Workers add messaging overhead and another lifecycle boundary;
            batching can add display latency. Rendering still happens on the
            main thread, so focused UI updates remain important. The
            loading-time blocking result does not isolate the worker’s effect on
            live interactions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="case-prose" aria-labelledby="measurement-title">
        <h2 id="measurement-title">How I measured the work</h2>
        <div class="case-body">
          <p>
            I used Lighthouse, network profiling and browser performance marks
            around bootstrap stages, including route loading, session
            availability, hydration and client initialisation. Testing included
            slow-4G conditions.
          </p>
          <p>
            The paint, blocking-time and transferred-data figures are combined
            results across the wider loading work. They do not isolate any
            single optimisation. Return navigation and the icon-package
            comparison are separate measurements.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <footer class="case-contact">
        <img src="/profile.jpeg" width="319" height="319" alt="" />
        <div>
          <h2>Let’s talk frontend.</h2>
          <p>Architecture, real-time interfaces and performance.</p>
          <a href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Email Jack ↗</a>
        </div>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
